// The boot seed: the owner's own company, as account #1.
//
// SEEDED, not special-cased, for the reason the portal is site #1: the
// Accounts app is where clients are managed, and an instance whose registry
// opens empty gives its owner nothing to recognize -- the first thing they
// should see is themselves, already in it. It also gives the four tie pickers
// a target on a cluster where nobody has typed anything yet.
//
// ===========================================================================
// AN AUTOMATION RATHER THAN A `seed` BLOCK, AND THE REASON IS MECHANICAL
// ===========================================================================
// The declarative seed path would have been the obvious home for this. It is
// not available: the SeedMaterializer derives the mutation it calls from the
// CONCEPT name -- `"create" + ucFirst(conceptName)` (seed_materializer.go) --
// so a `seed account self { ... }` block resolves to `createAccount`, which
// `dsl/identity/mutations.memql` has owned since long before this domain
// existed and which writes an entirely different concept. There is no hook to
// point it at `createClientAccount`, and renaming the concept to reach one
// would change `v1:accounts:account:self` -- the id the OS, the design record
// and the custom-domains epic all key on.
//
// So the seed takes the other shape the engine already has for a literal-id
// singleton, which is the one D3 names anyway: `bootstrapCluster`'s
// startup-triggered, absence-gated create (dsl/cluster/automations.memql).
// Same machinery, opposite gate -- see dsl/accounts/logic.memql.
/// Materialize the owner's own company on first startup
@trigger(event="system.startup")
automation seedSelfAccount {
  args {
    domain any
    node any
  }

  step decide {
    logic selfAccountAbsent (
      event
    )
  }

  // THE ID IS A LITERAL, and that is the accurate statement rather than a
  // shortcut: there is exactly one company that is this instance's owner.
  // It is also what makes the gate above meaningful -- a minted id would be
  // absent on every boot forever.
  //
  // `name` is a STARTER, not a claim. Nothing a boot can read knows what this
  // company is called, so the row says so plainly and the first-run card asks
  // (D7). `configuredAt` is left unwritten and that absence IS the card.
  //
  // `domain` comes from MEMQL_DOMAIN, forwarded onto the startup payload by
  // app/cluster.go. Every field binds an explicit key + coalesce, mirroring
  // bootstrapCluster: a multi-segment path evaluates to nil when absent, and a
  // nil argument for a required arg trips validation.
  step selfAccount {
    if steps.decide.result == true {
      mutation createClientAccount (
        accountId: "v1:accounts:account:self",
        name:      "My company",
        domain:    domain ?? ""
      )
    }
  }
}
